The repository diff establishes the new public type identity and the client, server, and DevTools mechanisms. Whether a real user observes rendering or lifecycle differences depends on applications actually using the experimental export and on the Activity modes and trees supplied at runtime.

The experimental `unstable_Activity` API now has its own React element symbol and reconciler work tag instead of being an alias for Offscreen. Client reconciliation represents Activity as a distinct outer fiber backed by an Offscreen child, server rendering sends it through the existing Offscreen renderer, and DevTools/component naming now exposes it as `Activity`.

Likely behavioral changes
  • Consumers of `unstable_Activity` now receive the dedicated `react.activity` element type rather than the Offscreen type. The new type is accepted as a valid React element type, so Activity has a distinct observable identity while remaining renderable.
  • On the client, an Activity element is represented by a dedicated Activity fiber whose `mode`, children, and ref are forwarded to an Offscreen child. Updates continue through that child rather than treating Activity itself as an Offscreen fiber.
  • Server rendering recognizes the new Activity type and renders it through the same Offscreen path used for Offscreen elements.
  • Developer tooling and component-stack naming can now identify the dedicated fiber as `Activity`, and DevTools treats that fiber as a non-user-code wrapper when deciding whether it should be inspected as an element.

